APS-003 — Canonical Policy Object (CPO)

Document ID: APS-003
Title: Canonical Policy Object (CPO)
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Policy Object (CPO), the standardized representation of policies within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The CPO establishes a governed, versioned, traceable, and interoperable policy model capable of defining behavioral constraints, permissions, obligations, prohibitions, and recommendations across the Atlas ecosystem.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Policy architecture
Policy structure
Policy semantics
Policy applicability
Policy effects
Policy obligations
Policy governance
Policy relationships

This specification applies to every policy within Atlas.

3. Canonical Definition

A Canonical Policy Object (CPO) is a specialization of the Canonical Atlas Object that formally defines governed behavioral rules applicable to one or more Atlas participants, objects, processes, or interactions.

Policies govern behavior.

Policies do not execute behavior.

4. Objectives

The Canonical Policy Object SHALL:

Standardize policy representation.
Preserve semantic consistency.
Support deterministic evaluation.
Enable explainable decisions.
Support governance.
Enable interoperability.
Support policy evolution.
Preserve traceability.
5. Inheritance

The Canonical Policy Object SHALL inherit every mandatory property of the Canonical Atlas Object.

Canonical Atlas Object
        │
        ▼
Canonical Policy Object
        │
 ┌──────┼──────────────┐
 ▼      ▼              ▼
Security Validation Workflow
 Policy    Policy      Policy

Future policy families SHALL extend this hierarchy.

6. Canonical Policy Structure

Every CPO SHALL define:

Property	Description
Policy ID	Globally unique identifier
Policy Name	Human-readable name
Policy Type	Canonical classification
Policy Version	Version identifier
Policy Status	Lifecycle state
Policy Owner	Governance owner
Policy Scope	Applicability boundary
Policy Priority	Evaluation precedence
7. Applicability

Every policy SHALL explicitly define where it applies.

Applicability MAY reference:

Canonical Atlas Objects
Object types
Engineering domains
Organizations
Projects
Workflows
Roles
Geographic regions
Regulatory jurisdictions
Time periods

Policies SHALL NOT rely upon implicit applicability.

8. Conditions

A policy SHALL define one or more evaluation conditions.

Each condition SHALL include:

Condition identifier
Evaluation expression
Required context
Applicable object(s)
Dependencies

Conditions SHALL be independently identifiable.

9. Policy Effects

Atlas defines the following canonical policy effects.

Effect	Description
Permit	Action is allowed
Deny	Action is prohibited
Require	Action becomes mandatory
Recommend	Action is advised
Warn	Advisory notification
Escalate	Human or higher authority review required

Effects SHALL be deterministic.

10. Obligations

Policies MAY generate obligations.

Each obligation SHALL define:

Responsible participant
Required action
Completion criteria
Deadline
Escalation rules

Obligations SHALL remain traceable until satisfied or withdrawn.

11. Policy Relationships

Policies MAY define relationships including:

Relationship	Purpose
Depends On	Requires another policy
Extends	Specializes another policy
Overrides	Replaces another policy
Conflicts With	Declares incompatibility
References	Uses another policy

Relationships SHALL conform to ADS-011 — Relationship Model.

12. Policy Metadata

Every policy SHALL include:

Canonical metadata
Governance metadata
Security classification
Version information
Lifecycle state
Provenance
Compliance references

Metadata SHALL conform to ADS-010 — Metadata Model.

13. Policy Lifecycle

Every policy SHALL progress through the canonical lifecycle.

Draft
   │
Review
   │
Approved
   │
Published
   │
Active
   │
Deprecated
   │
Retired

Policies SHALL NOT be modified after publication.

Changes SHALL create new versions.

14. Policy Governance

Every CPO SHALL define:

Owner
Steward
Reviewer
Approver
Governance domain
Approval workflow
Audit requirements

Governance SHALL remain attached throughout the policy lifecycle.

15. Policy Quality

Every policy SHALL satisfy:

Correctness
Completeness
Consistency
Determinism
Explainability
Traceability
Maintainability
Testability

Quality SHALL be measurable.

16. Conformance

A Canonical Policy Object conforms if it:

Inherits the CAO contract.
Defines mandatory policy properties.
Specifies applicability.
Defines evaluation conditions.
Declares policy effects.
Supports governance.
Supports lifecycle management.
17. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Adaptive policies.
AI-generated policy recommendations.
Semantic policy discovery.
Dynamic applicability.
Federated policy catalogs.
Domain-specific policy profiles.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical policy semantics.

